{
  "abstract": "A rational point on the diagonal cover of an order-four Diophantine construction need not lift to any nondegenerate base, even when every completion admits such lifts. We prove this for the ratio $\\lambda=54/65$ and the diagonal point $(v′,G′,W′)=(1098/845,7/169,16758/54925)$. Fixing this point and varying the base gives five reciprocal-square conditions, equivalent away from the boundary to six linear-square conditions. We determine all rational points of the resulting genus-$49$ cover: they are the $64$ sign choices above the degenerate parameter $x=0$. The proof passes to a genus-two quotient whose two elliptic factors have rank one. Exact valuation partitions retain the full six-square restrictions and reduce the non-p local-height data to $32$ possibilities. Bielliptic quadratic Chabauty at $29$ and $31$, together with full-group finite reductions, leaves disjoint sets of extra height indices. Independent implementations certify disk coverage, true-root error bounds, square filtering, formal logarithms and every finite reduction; coefficient calculations are repeated on conservative balls containing the actual analytic roots. The computation identifies standard BP/Sage height-series and eclib saturation inputs explicitly. The result excludes this one diagonal point across all moving bases without a height bound. It does not exclude other diagonal points at the same ratio, other construction architectures, or rational Diophantine septuples.",
